Excercise 2:


17 / 5 = 3 (int)
17 % 5 = 2 (int)
17.0 / 5 = 2.0 (double)
5 % 17 = 5 (int)

-7 % 3 = -1 (int)
7 / 2 * 2 = 6 (int)
7 / 2.0 = 3
1 + 2 * 3 % 4 = 3 (int)

int a = 7, b = 8;
double avg = (a + b) / 2;
System.out.println(avg);

Answer: 7.0 is printed as both a and b are declared as integers.

Fix: cast "a" and "b" to double or force "(double)" in the avg calculation

Excercise 3:
int n = 5;
int a = n++;
int b = n--;
int c = --n;
int d = ++n;
System.out.println(a + " " + b + " " + c + " " + d + " " + n);

Output: 5,6,4,5,5
Note: if "x = ""++" then add 1 to the inital variable, x becomes the original value of the initial variable

int orders = 10;
orders = orders++;
System.out.println(orders);
Answer: 10

Excercise 4:

b = true
c= false
x = 4, y= 9

a) x < y && c (= false)

(b) !(x > y) || c (= true)
(c) x == 4 || y / 0 > 1 (does this crash? why not?) (=true, does not crash as x==4 is already true so it does not need to continue to y/0)
(d) b && !c && x % 2 == 0 (=true)
(e) !b || (y >= 9 && x != 4) (= false)

Excercise 5:

1. Breakfast is served when hour is from 7 to 10 inclusive
double time = ...
if (time <= 10.00 || time >= 7.00)

2. Free delivery if the total is over £20, or if a premium member spends at least £10
double total = ...
double delivery = ...
boolean premiumMember = ...
if (total > 20.00 || ((premiumMember) && total >= 10.00))

3. The meal deal applies to students spending at least £5, and to everyone during happy hour
  (15:00 to 16:59).

  boolean student = ...
  double total = ...
  double time = ...
  if ((time >= 15.00 || time <= 16.59) || ((student) && total >= 5.00))

  4.A student writes if (7 <= age <= 12) for the children's menu. Why does Java reject it?
    Rewrite it.

    You need 2 comparisons as java cannot compare a variable to 2 values at once
    if (age <= 12 && age >= 7)


Excercise 6:
// (a) free delivery
if (total > 20); {
freeDelivery = true;
}
// (b) member discount
boolean isMember = false;
if (isMember = true) {
price = price * 0.9;
}
// (c) loyalty reward
if (points >= 100)
reward = "Free drink";
points -= 100;

(a) ";" after the if statement stops anything running after it

(a) if (total > 20) {
freeDelivery = true;
}

(b) isMember is set to false right before a condition checking its status which would mean members never get the reduction

(b) if (isMember = true) {
price *= 0.9;
}

(c) No {} to denote what is in the if statement

(c) if (points >= 100) {
reward = "Free drink";
points -= 100;
}

Excercise 7:
Write a program which asks the user to enter two integers and displays "greater" if the first one is
greater than the second one and "smaller or equal" otherwise. Then change it to display three
different messages: "greater", "equal" or "smaller

Excercise 8:
Write a program which asks the user to enter their age and displays "Adult" if it is 18 or over,
"Underage" otherwise, and "Invalid age" if it is below 0 or above 120. Test it with every value in the
table

Excercise 9:
Write a program which asks the user to enter a mark and displays the grade: A+ for 80 and above,
A for 70+, B for 60+, C for 50+, D for 40+, F otherwise, and "Invalid mark" outside 0 to 100. Before
you start: what goes wrong if you test mark >= 40 first?

Excercise 10:
Write a program which asks the user to enter a bill in pence (e.g. 437) and displays it as pounds
and pence ("£4 and 37p"), using only / and %.

Excercise 11:
Write a program which asks the user to enter a month of the year (as a String) and displays the
corresponding number (JANUARY=1, FEBRUARY=2, ...). Use a switch on the String, and display
"Unknown month" for anything else. Hint: s.toUpperCase() lets the user type "january" too.

Excercise 12:

// Version 1
  if (temp > 25) System.out.print("Iced ");
  else if (temp > 15) System.out.print("Flat ");
  else System.out.print("Hot");
  // Version 2
  if (temp > 25) System.out.print("Iced ");
  if (temp > 15) System.out.print("Flat ");
  else System.out.print("Hot");


  Temp: 5 -> "Hot" -> "Hot"
  Temp: 18 -> "Flat" -> "Flat"
  Temp: 30 -> "Iced" -> "Iced" "Flat"

  Version 1 is only different since temp > 15 only runs if temp > 25 is false

