  System.out.println("B"); }

  Excercise 13:
  Give the final price (in pence) for each size: 'S', 'M', 'L' and 'X'. Is the missing break a bug or a
  deliberate design? Rewrite it so a small costs 200, a medium 250 and a large 300

  int price = 200;
  switch (size) {
  case 'S': price += 0;
  break'
  case 'M': price += 50;
  break;
  case 'L': price += 50;
  break;
  default: price = 0;
  }

  Excercise 14:

  Write a program which asks whether the customer is a student (y/n), the order total in pounds and
  the hour (0 to 23). Display "Meal deal applies" if the customer is a student spending at least £5, or
  the hour is 15 or 16. Otherwise display "No meal deal".

  Excercise 15:
  A drink costs £4.00. Customers under 16 get 50% off; otherwise, customers with a student ID get
  20% off (only the single best percentage discount applies). On Wednesday (day 3) everyone gets a
  further £0.50 off. Ask for the age, student ID (y/n) and day (1 to 7), and display the price with
  System.out.printf("£%.2f%n", price);. Extension: use a switch on the day to display its
  name too, e.g. "Wednesday: £2.70"

  Excercise 16:
  Write a program which asks the user to enter a month (1 to 12) and a year, and displays the
   number of days in that month. Use a switch with stacked cases, and a leap-year condition for
   February: divisible by 4 but not by 100, unless also divisible by 400. Test with 2/2024 → 29,
   2/2023 → 28, 2/1900 → 28, 2/2000 → 29, 4/2025 → 30, 13/2025 → "Invalid month"